<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Tondibi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1591">1591</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Tondibi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Tondibi was fought on 12 March 1591 near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a>, where a Moroccan expeditionary force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judar-pasha">Judar Pasha</a>, armed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebuses</a> and light <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, routed the much larger army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askia-ishaq-ii">Askia Ishaq II</a>. The battle showed the overwhelming advantage that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons gave a small force. It led directly to the collapse of the Songhai Empire.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> had been the dominant power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> for more than a century, controlling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sudan">Western Sudan</a> from the headwaters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a> to what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, but succession conflicts after the death of Askia Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> in 1586 had weakened the empire. To the north the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadi-dynasty">Saadi dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> was at the height of its power after repelling a Portuguese invasion at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Battle of Alcácer Quibir</a> in 1578, yet the cost of its defences had brought it close to bankruptcy.</p><p>Seeking new resources, Sultan Ahmad I al-Mansur turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a>, wrongly believing that their wealth came from gold mines. Many advisers warned that war on another Muslim nation was unlawful, but he set their objections aside and in October 1590 sent an army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judar-pasha">Judar Pasha</a>, a Spanish eunuch captured as a child.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The expedition travelled with 8,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, 1,000 packhorses and eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> on 28 February 1591 after a four-month journey, and razed the salt mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taghaza">Taghaza</a>. Judar's army of 1,500 light cavalry and 2,500 infantry, many armed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebuses</a>, faced a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> force estimated at 18,000 cavalry and 9,700 foot soldiers by the Tarikh al-fattash and at up to 80,000 men by other accounts, strong in cavalry but without firearms.</p><p>The armies met at Tankondibogho, near Tondibi, just north of Gao. A stampede of 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> meant to break the Moroccan lines was turned back by the noise of the guns. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> infantry and then their cavalry were cut down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire, and the rearguard fought hand to hand until it was killed. The battle took about two hours; according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a>, some Songhai soldiers sat on their shields rather than flee and were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Judar sacked Gao, already evacuated by its residents, and moved on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a>. Morocco could not hold the vast region, and after a decade of sporadic fighting it splintered into dozens of smaller kingdoms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> moved east to their surviving province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dendi">Dendi</a> and kept their tradition alive there for two and a half centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Tondibi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Ahmad al-Mansur sent an expedition of a few thousand men, many of them musketeers, under the Spanish-born eunuch Judar Pasha across the Sahara; it defeated the much larger Songhai army at the <b>Battle of Tondibi</b> in March 1591 and occupied Gao and Timbuktu, creating the Pashalik of Timbuktu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the <b>Battle of Tondibi</b> a much larger Songhai army was routed in a single engagement, which showed how far gunpowder weapons could favour a small, well-equipped force over a larger army armed in the older way.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1591 a Moroccan army of a few thousand men, many of them armed with arquebuses and led by Judar Pasha, crossed the Sahara and defeated the far larger army of the Songhai Empire at <b>Tondibi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>After the defeat of the Songhai Empire at <b>Tondibi</b> in 1591, Askia Nuh led the survivors south down the Niger into the province of Dendi, in present-day south-western Niger and northern Benin.</blockquote><a href="/state/dendi-kingdom">Dendi kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1591 an army sent by the Saadi Sultanate under Judar Pasha crossed the Sahara and defeated the Songhai Empire at <b>Tondibi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/pashalik-of-timbuktu">Pashalik of Timbuktu</a></li></ul></section>
